## Data Model and API Design (Exercise 3.3)

### Task data model

| Field | Purpose | Example |
|---|---|---|
| `id` | Unique task identifier | `"task-7f3a"` |
| `title` | Task name | `"Submit lab report"` |
| `description` | Additional details | `"Complete the results section and references."` |
| `priority` | Importance of the task; allowed values and default require agreement | `"medium"` |
| `status` | Completion state; exact representation requires agreement | `"incomplete"` |
| `due_date` | Target date; date-only versus date-time behavior requires agreement | `"2025-04-18"` |

### API design

Use a `tasks` resource with JSON request and response bodies. The API supports the required create, view, update, and delete operations:

| Method and path | Purpose | Example |
|---|---|---|
| `POST /tasks` | Create a task | Request body: `{ "title": "Submit lab report", "description": "Complete the results section.", "priority": "medium", "status": "incomplete", "due_date": "2025-04-18" }`; return the created task, including its `id`. |
| `GET /tasks` | List tasks | Return an array of task objects; an empty collection returns `[]`. |
| `GET /tasks/{id}` | View task details | `GET /tasks/task-7f3a` returns that task. |
| `PUT /tasks/{id}` | Update task fields | Send the updated task fields; return the saved task. |
| `DELETE /tasks/{id}` | Delete a task | `DELETE /tasks/task-7f3a`; report success or failure. |

Validation rules, priority options, status representation, date semantics, and error response details remain subject to stakeholder agreement.

## Design Review (Exercise 3.4)

### Review findings

- **Testability:** The CRUD acceptance criteria are generally testable, including success, validation failure, empty-list, cancellation, and delete-failure cases. However, several requirements are not yet objectively verifiable: NFR-01 uses “understandable,” NFR-02 does not define how to detect partial updates, and NFR-03, NFR-04, NFR-05, and NFR-06 lack measurable targets or agreed conditions. FR-01 and FR-03 depend on validation rules that have not been decided; FR-05 needs agreed persistence and retention expectations. Finalize these details and define measurable acceptance tests before implementation.
- **Data model:** Each listed field has a stated purpose. Clarify whether `status` is the same as “completion status” throughout the requirements and stories, and agree on its representation and allowed values. Specify identifier generation and whether an ownership field is needed if the application is multi-student; authentication and ownership are intentionally undecided.
- **API alignment:** `POST`, collection and item `GET`, `PUT`, and `DELETE` map to task creation, viewing, updating, and deletion. Define whether `PUT` requires a complete task representation, and specify success and error responses (including validation failures and unavailable IDs) so the API can be tested consistently.
- **Invalid or missing data:** The criteria require rejecting invalid or missing values without creating or altering a task, and edge cases identify likely inputs. The actual required fields, limits, formats, priority/status values, and date rules remain open, so validation behavior is not yet fully testable. Agree on these rules and define how API and interface errors identify the issue.
- **Scope:** No unjustified feature is specified. Sharing, reminders, recurrence, sorting, search, and integrations are explicitly excluded or pending approval. Treat concurrent edits and large lists in the edge-case list as review considerations, not committed features or performance requirements, until stakeholders approve them.

